<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#percheron">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Percheron</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Percheron is a breed of heavy draught horse from the Perche region of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, usually grey or black. Developed from local horses, probably with some Arab blood, it became famous in the 19th century for pulling coaches, guns and farm machinery, and was exported in large numbers to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. It remains one of the best-known draught breeds.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/percheron.jpg" alt="Percheron" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eponimm · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cheval-percheron-gris_SDA2014_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Percheron comes from the valley of the Huisne in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, part of the former province of Perche, which gave the breed its name. Its exact origins are unknown. Foundation stock may have included mares captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bretons">Bretons</a>, or Barb stallions brought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moors">Moors</a> in the 8th century. Barb stallions were certainly crossed with local mares in the 8th century, and more Oriental blood came with the Comte du Perche on his return from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusades</a>. Breed historians agree that the terrain and climate of Perche shaped it most.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the age of the armoured knight, breeders sought horses able to pull heavy stagecoaches at a fast trot, the Diligence Horse, gray being preferred for its visibility at night. When railways replaced the coaches a heavier type arose for farm work and hauling goods. The breed nearly became extinct when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horse-breeding">horse breeding</a> was suppressed during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>. Jean le Blanc, foaled in 1823, is the stallion to which all Percherons trace their ancestry, and the breed grew to a draft horse averaging about 910 kilograms. The French stud book was created in 1893.</p><p>Edward Harris II of Moorestown, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a>, first imported Percherons to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1839. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a> demand for large draft horses grew, and about 7,500 were exported in the 1880s. In the 1930s Percherons made up over 70 per cent of purebred draft horses in the United States, but mechanisation after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> caused a steep decline, and only 85 were registered there in 1954.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Percheron is the best known and most numerous French draft breed. It was used to improve the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardennes">Ardennes</a> and Vladimir Heavy Draft horses and to create the Spanish-Norman. By the end of the 19th century Percherons made up most of the driving horses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. They are still used for draft work and forestry, and for parades, sleigh rides and carriage work.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Percheron” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Perche was a land of forests, iron and horse breeding, later famous for the <b>Percheron</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-perche">County of Perche</a></li></ul></section>
